CATINTRO={
'wedding-album-12x36':"The 12x36 inch spread is the standard wedding album size in most Indian studios: opened flat it is three times wider than it is tall, which gives room for one strong portrait plus a row of candid frames. Every file in this category is a full double-page sheet, usually 10800x3600 pixels at 300 DPI, with photo placeholders built as clipping masks so you can drop in pictures and resize them without touching the artwork. Use the tags below to narrow down by style (royal, floral, golden, minimal) or by ceremony. Before you start, confirm the exact sheet size and bleed with your lab; most digital labs print 12x36 sheets from flattened sRGB JPG files.",
'album-background':"Album backgrounds are the textures, patterns and soft scenes that sit behind your photo frames. A good background sets the colour mood of a spread without competing with faces, so most designs here keep the centre calm and put detail near the edges. They are prepared at album-sheet proportions (mostly 12x36) and high resolution, so they stay sharp in print. Pair one background family across a whole album for a consistent look, and use a Hue/Saturation adjustment layer if you need to match a client's colour theme.",
'album-cover':"The album cover is the first thing a client sees, so it needs one clean hero photo, readable names and a date. The cover designs here are set up for the front of the book (commonly 12x18 for a 12x36 album) with editable text layers and a single main photo frame. If your lab prints a wrap-around cover, ask for its template with spine width and wrap margin, then place the front design on the right-hand panel. Keep text at least half an inch inside the trim line.",
'album-12x30':"12x30 is a slightly shorter panoramic sheet than 12x36 and many labs print it at a lower price, which makes it popular for budget packages and parent albums. These files are built at true 12x30 proportions (for example 9000x3600 pixels at 300 DPI), so you do not have to crop or stretch a 12x36 layout. Check the centre fold position, keep faces away from it, and export a flattened sRGB JPG at full size for printing.",
'album-18x24':"18x24 is a large-format size used for premium coffee-table albums, big family portraits and display spreads. Because the pages are bigger, these PSDs use fewer, larger photo frames and need high-resolution source images; a 24 inch wide print at 300 DPI is 7200 pixels across. Use your sharpest photos here, avoid upscaling small JPGs, and proof at 100% zoom before sending to the lab.",
'birthday-baby-album':"Birthday and baby albums call for brighter colours, playful shapes and room for cake, decorations and family groups. This category covers first birthday albums, kids party spreads, baby shower and newborn layouts. Most files are 12x36 sheets with editable name and age text. Plan the album as a story (preparations, cake cutting, games, family photos, thank-you page) and keep the palette to two or three colours so the pages feel connected.",
'studio-background':"Studio background PSDs let you place a portrait on a clean, professional backdrop without a physical set. They are useful for passport and ID studios, kids portraits, festival prints and quick event photos. Cut the subject out with Select Subject, refine the hair edge with Select and Mask, then place it above the background and match brightness with a Curves adjustment clipped to the subject.",
'wedding-invitation':"Wedding invitation and save-the-date PSDs let studios offer matching stationery with their albums and videos. Names, dates, venues and family details are on editable text layers. For print, confirm the card size and bleed with your printer; for WhatsApp and Instagram sharing, export a 1080 pixel wide JPG or PNG. Always double-check spellings and dates with the family before printing.",
'album-12x18':"12x18 sheets are used for compact albums, pre-wedding books and album covers. The smaller format works best with fewer photos and more white space. These files are set up at 12x18 proportions so they print without cropping; confirm the bleed with your lab before export.",
}
